{
  "note": "Shared conformance corpus for the JSON-Schema `format` keyword, asserted-v1 subset (uuid, ipv4, ipv6, date, time, date-time). Each pair has an id, a `format` (one of those six), a `value` (the string under test), and `expect_valid` (the pinned check's verdict: true = the generator-owned check MUST accept, false = MUST reject). The pinned check is a portable anchored regex (RE2-safe: no lookaround, no backreferences) PLUS, for the temporal formats, a shared integer-arithmetic calendar predicate (month 01-12, day within the month's Gregorian length incl. leap-year Feb 29). Pinned RFC 3339 edge decisions: leap second :60 REJECTED (the materialized grammar narrows the seconds group to [0-5][0-9] because no target's native temporal type can hold :60 -- measured: Go, JavaScript, Python and Java all reject 23:59:60Z and all four accept 23:59:59Z; RFC 3339 does permit :60, and the wider :60-accepting grammar is specified to live behind the `string` opt-out, which is not implemented yet -- flip time-second-60-leap and dt-leap-second back to true when it ships); date-time offset REQUIRED (Z or +-HH:MM); materialized time/date-time offsets LIMITED to -14:00 through +14:00 inclusive, so hour 14 permits minute 00 only (real-world offsets span -12:00..+14:00, and +-14:00 is the narrowest range every target's native carrier accepts -- .NET DateTimeOffset caps at +-14:00); -00:00 ACCEPTED; fractional seconds ANY precision (RFC 3339 sets no limit; the contract is accept every width and TRUNCATE only where the carrier is a materialized native type, which is the bounded loss P1 exception (b) allows -- for date-time: Python datetime to microseconds, Go time.Time / Java OffsetDateTime / TS Temporal to nanoseconds, and the TS string default not at all; for time: Python to microseconds and Go time.Time to nanoseconds, while Java holds time in a String and TS holds it in a string in every mode, so neither of those two may truncate it -- so do NOT cap the grammar to make a target that rejects long fractions agree; see dt-frac-high-precision and samples/python/tests/test_temporal.py test_sub_second_precision_is_accepted_at_every_width); T/Z case-insensitive; time may be local (no offset) per RFC 3339 partial-time (the corpus tests this). Runners implement the pinned check, never a native typed parser, so all seven runtimes must agree value-for-value (P1). This resolves format spec open question #2.",
